Chapter: BrotliHostile
  cites Compress chapter Brotli

 Track D item 19, the Brotli half, and this chapter is the one where the
 census verdict was "best defended by inspection". That turned out to be
 right, so this arm adds no guards: it exists because "defended by
 inspection" and "defended" are different claims, and the only hostile
 vector the chapter had was the empty stream through brotli-valid.

 Guarded-or-not aside, it qualifies under red's ruling of 2026-08-16: four
 in-tree harnesses run it, plus build/brotli-interop-test.ps1 and
 brotli-read-test.ps1.

 WHAT THIS ARM DOES NOT DO, first, because a reader who assumes otherwise
 will trust it for something it never tested. It does NOT exercise the
 guards the census credits this chapter with. Measured by ablation
 2026-08-16: deleting the brotli-valid gate at :1834 moved NO row, and
 widening the dist < 1 refusal at :2405 to an unreachable bound moved NO
 row. Hostile HEADERS and truncated streams do not decode far enough to
 reach a distance command. Reaching those guards needs a crafted bitstream
 that gets through the meta-block header and the Huffman tables first, which
 is a fuzz-corpus job and is not done.

 Two things follow, and they are the findings rather than the arm.
 brotli-valid:2502 is not load-bearing for anything constructible here: its
 w <= 24 half is UNREACHABLE by inspection, since brotli-read-wbits:313 can
 only answer 16, 17, 9..15 or 18..24, and its w < 10 half fires only for
 wbits 9, where the decoder independently answers the empty list anyway.
 And brotli-max-dist-w:325 clamps a distance to the bytes actually PRODUCED,
 which is why reek's Deflate guard covering Brotli's :2408 call site never
 fires here: the non-dictionary path cannot reach a negative source.

 What the arm DOES establish is termination, which nothing else in the tree
 asserts: brotli-test is a round trip through our own encoder, and the
 interop harness carries one corrupted-stream control. Rows 6 and 7 run the
 decoder end to end on bytes we did not write and require it to stop.

 Rows 1 to 3 are the positive controls. Rows 4 and 5 report an exact length,
 because both are derivable from the source; rows 6 and 7 report a bound,
 because a mutated stream's exact output is not.

Section: Helpers

  list-equal : List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  list-equal (a) (b) (i) (len) =
    if i >= len then True
    else if list-at a i == list-at b i then list-equal a b (i + 1) len
    else False

  check-rt : List Integer, Text -> Text
  check-rt (input) (label) =
    let c = brotli-compress input
    in let d = brotli-decompress c
    in let ok = if list-length d == list-length input then list-equal input d 0 (list-length input) else False
    in label & "=" & if ok then "pass" else "fail"

  make-repeated : Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  make-repeated (val) (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else make-repeated val n (i + 1) (list-push acc val)

  make-skew : Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  make-skew (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else make-skew n (i + 1) (list-push acc (if i - (i / 8) * 8 == 0 then 66 else 65))

  take-n : List Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  take-n (xs) (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else if i >= list-length xs then acc
    else take-n xs n (i + 1) (list-push acc (list-at xs i))

  show-len : List Integer, Text -> Text
  show-len (out) (label) = label & " len=" & show (list-length out)

 A mutated stream's exact output is not derivable from the definition, so
 these rows assert what the code actually promises: that the walk stops.
 An arm that asserted a captured number would be an oracle copied from a run
 of the code under test.

  show-bounded : List Integer, Text -> Text
  show-bounded (out) (label) =
    label & " terminated bounded=" & (if list-length out < 1000000 then "True" else "False")

 Row 5's header, derived from brotli-read-wbits:313 rather than guessed. Bit
 0 set sends it past the wbits=16 shorthand; bits 1..3 zero make n zero; bits
 4..6 equal to one make m one, so wbits is 8 + 1 = 9. brotli-valid:2507
 refuses anything below 10, so the whole stream is refused before a single
 symbol is decoded. That is byte 1 + 16 = 17.

  wbits-nine : List Integer = [17]

 Row 6 keeps a LEGAL window (bit 0 clear is the wbits=16 shorthand) and then
 hands the decoder nothing but set bits, so brotli-valid passes and the
 refusal, if any, has to come from the decode itself.

  valid-header-garbage : List Integer = [0, 255, 255, 255, 255, 255, 255, 255, 255, 255, 255, 255]

  truncated : List Integer
  truncated =
    let full = brotli-compress (make-skew 600 0 [])
    in take-n full (list-length full / 2) 0 []

Section: Body

 Each row runs between a heap mark and a restore, following brotli-test: a
 decode of garbage is exactly the thing that allocates, and the rows must not
 pay for each other.

  row : Text -> [Console] Integer
  row (t) = act
    let mark = __heap-save
    in act
      print-line-uni t
      let d = __heap-restore mark
      in 0
    end
  end

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    a <- row (check-rt [] "1-empty")
    b <- row (check-rt (make-repeated 65 200 0 []) "2-uniform-200")
    c <- row (check-rt (make-skew 600 0 []) "3-skew-600")
    d <- row (show-len (brotli-decompress []) "4-empty-input")
    e <- row (show-len (brotli-decompress wbits-nine) "5-wbits-nine")
    f <- row (show-bounded (brotli-decompress valid-header-garbage) "6-valid-header-garbage")
    print-line-uni (show-bounded (brotli-decompress truncated) "7-truncated")
  end

